der the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{16}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.At $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14073830/state.json" "file_sha256" "c6af7f2b4c93754be1006c1ddfd7f72aa981f1dc7d370a0d2a7241e65e645341"} {"question" "q-343443" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-1-\\frac{16}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-n\\pi )=(-1)^{n}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-1-\\frac{16}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\ 3 & =-1-\\frac{16}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n} \\\\ 4 & =-\\frac{16}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14